# API, auth, and authorization rules

## API design

Consistent resource naming, correct HTTP methods, meaningful status codes, Zod-validated
request payloads, and a single consistent error structure. Version the API only if a real
need appears.

`200` success · `201` created · `204` no content · `400` malformed · `401` unauthenticated
· `403` unauthorized · `404` not found · `409` conflict · `422` validation failure ·
`500` unexpected

Never return internal database errors, stack traces, or sensitive internals to clients.
Log actionable diagnostic context server-side, without secrets.

## Validation

Backend validation is authoritative; frontend validation is UX only. Validate at the
trusted boundary: required fields, types, ranges, formats, enum values, identifiers, and
ownership. Never rely on a client-side check alone.

## Authentication

Hash passwords with bcrypt — never store plaintext. Protect authenticated routes, manage
session and token expiry securely, implement logout, and use secure defaults.

## Authorization

Enforce roles server-side on every route. Never trust a client-side role check.

**Citizen** — manage own account, create own intake, view own intakes and consultation
requests, browse permitted lawyer profiles, request a consultation.

**Lawyer** — manage own permitted profile fields, view consultation requests sent to them,
view the associated intake only when authorised, update consultation status.

**Admin** — manage users and categories, approve or suspend lawyer profiles, view
administrative records required for platform management.

Every object-level route checks ownership as well as role. Predictable IDs must not expose
another user's intake (see `SEC-LG-001`, `SEC-LG-002`, `SEC-LG-008` in
`docs/04-testing.md`).

## Consultation workflow

Statuses: `AWAITING_PAYMENT`, `PENDING`, `ACCEPTED`, `DECLINED`, `COMPLETED`, `CANCELLED`.
A client proposes `scheduledAt` when booking (FR-019). Accepting requires a Google Meet
URL; Google Calendar is a template link, not OAuth sync (TD-027).

## Matching

Filtering and ranking are deterministic application logic, not AI output, so they can be
tested and explained (NFR-007). Practice-area match carries the highest weight, location
and availability are secondary, and active/approved status is a mandatory eligibility
condition. Every recommendation carries a human-readable reason, e.g. "Recommended because
this lawyer lists Employment Law as a practice area and is available in your region."
Never claim a lawyer is "the best".

## Privacy

Legal intake text is sensitive. Collect only what the MVP needs, avoid logging full intake
text, never log passwords or tokens, and restrict lawyer and admin access to what the
workflow actually requires.
